The article states the source of every figure – for example, ‘according to a statement from the local council’. {{1}}
A huge headline, a dramatic celebrity photo, lots of exclamation marks – attention is all that matters. {{2}}
An anonymous account spreads a shocking story – no legal notice, no source, and nobody checks before posting. {{3}}
The opinion piece is clearly labelled as opinion and kept separate from the news report. {{4}}
The editorial team openly publishes a correction after making a mistake. {{5}}
The story is told with dramatic emphasis and emotion – the core is true, but everything sounds more dramatic than it was. {{6}}
The post has hundreds of thousands of shares, but nobody can say who originally wrote it. {{7}}
‘You won’t believe what happened next!’ – the curiosity-driven headline is mainly intended to generate clicks. {{8}}
A personal account shares an old storm photo, claiming it is a current image – nobody has checked it. {{9}}
